Canonicalization

A canonicalized system is declaratively defined with NixOS, uses an ephemeral root filesystem with explicitly preserved state, and has a home directory managed as a Git repository with a whitelist `.gitignore`. The home repository organizes projects in a Go-style path layout (for example, `github.com/owner/project`). Each project is a Git submodule and a Nix flake with its own whitelist `.gitignore`. Flake repositories follow a small, purpose-built subset of the https://github.com/numtide/blueprint directory layout: a README explains the repository's intent, packages contain a single source file, tests describe the expected behavior, and code implements that behavior. Conventional formatters and strict linters are applied.

This repository provides tools for creating and maintaining that state.

Home repository:

```sh
cd
git init
printf '*\n!/.gitignore\n!/.gitmodules\n' > .gitignore # also add other required files/directories like .ssh
git add .gitignore
git commit -m 'Initialize home repository'
```

Project submodule:

```sh
# Create the empty remote before starting this workflow.
git submodule add --force git@github.com:owner/demo.git ~/github.com/owner/demo
cd ~/github.com/owner/demo
git canonicalization init . # initializes a flake repository with these conventions
git canonicalization add <type> <name> # scaffolds a package in the selected flake repository
nix fmt # runs conventional formatters and strict linters
nix flake check # verifies the canonical structure and runs builds and tests
```
